-- title:
--   Supersingular base point with endomorphism dictionary and endomorphism-ring export
-- statement:
--   Fix natural numbers $r,\bar r,N,n$ with $r$ and $\bar r$ prime, $N \neq 0$, together with the **numerical hypotheses** $\bar r \neq r$, $r \nmid N$, $\bar r \nmid N$, $N$ squarefree, $n \geq 3$, $r \nmid n$, $\bar r \nmid n$ and $\gcd(n,N) = 1$.
--
--   **Base rings.** $\mathcal O$ is a characteristic-zero commutative domain which is a discrete valuation ring in which $2$ is a unit, $\pi \in \mathcal O$ is an irreducible element, $\mathcal O$ is adically complete for the ideal $(\pi)$, the residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements, and $(r) = (\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$ (so $\mathcal O$ is absolutely unramified over $\mathbb Z_r$). $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero field which is a fraction field of $\mathcal O$. $\mathit{Onr}$ is a characteristic-zero commutative domain and an $\mathcal O$-algebra, adically complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$, this ideal being maximal; moreover every element of $\mathit{Onr}$ satisfies some monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), and every monic polynomial over $\mathit{Onr}$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`). Thus $k_0 := \mathit{Onr}/(\pi)$ plays the role of an algebraic closure of $\mathbb F_r$; it is the base ring of the conclusion throughout.
--
--   **The indefinite side.** $a,b \in \mathbb Q$ satisfy `IsIndefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b r rbar`: $0 < a$ or $0 < b$, and for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathbb Z$ the completion $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b] \otimes_{\mathbb Q} \mathbb Q_v$ is a division ring precisely when $v$ contains $r$ or $\bar r$. $\Lambda \subseteq \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ is a $\mathbb Z$-submodule which is a maximal order (an order — containing $1$, multiplicatively closed, spanning the algebra over $\mathbb Q$, finitely generated — maximal among orders), and `hΛℤ` asserts that every rational integer lies in $\Lambda$. The map $\mathrm{coord} : \Lambda \to \mathbb W(\mathbb F_{r^2})^2$ (values in `Zp2 r × Zp2 r`, the Witt vectors of the quadratic Galois field of characteristic $r$) satisfies `IsOrderCoord Λ r coord`: it is additive, sends $1$ to $(1,0)$, is multiplicative for the twisted rule $\mathrm{coord}(mm') = (\alpha\alpha' + r\,\beta\,\varphi(\beta'),\ \alpha\beta' + \beta\,\varphi(\alpha'))$ with $\varphi$ the Witt-vector Frobenius, is injective, has dense image (every pair of residues modulo $r^k$ is attained), and is trace-compatible: if $m + \bar m$ is the rational integer $n'$ then the first component of $\mathrm{coord}(m)$ plus its Frobenius equals $n'$.
--
--   **The definite side.** $a_1,b_1 \in \mathbb Q$ satisfy `IsDefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt rbar`: $a_1 < 0$, $b_1 < 0$, and $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \otimes_{\mathbb Q} \mathbb Q_v$ is a division ring precisely when $v$ contains $\bar r$. Inside this algebra, $\Lambda_1$ is a maximal order, $R_1$ is an Eichler order of level $N$ (an intersection of two maximal orders, of relative index $N$ in the first), and $R_1 \leq \Lambda_1$. Further, $\iota_0 : \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \to M_2(K_0)$ is an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map, $v$ is a height-one prime of $\mathbb Z$ containing $r$, and $\Gamma_t$ is a subgroup of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^\times$ whose elements are exactly those of [`CerednikDrinfeld.CosetGraph.awayUnits R₁ v`](def/CerednikDrinfeld_CosetGraphAtPrime.html#L37), i.e. the units that lie, at every height-one prime $w \neq v$, in the subgroup generated by the local box units of $R_1$ at $w$ (the group $R_1[1/r]^\times$).
--
--   **Conclusion.** There exist a fake elliptic curve $A_0$ of type `FakeEllipticCurve Λ N k₀` (a smooth proper scheme $A_0 \to \operatorname{Spec} k_0$ with connected fibres of dimension $2$, a commutative relative group law $L$, an action `act` of $\Lambda$ by endomorphisms over the base which is additive and anti-multiplicative in the diagrammatic sense and satisfies the tangent-trace condition, together with level-$N$ data `lev : C ⟶ A`), a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X_0$ of type `FormalODModule r k₀` (a commutative two-dimensional formal group law $F$ with an action of `Zp2 r` by law endomorphisms and an endomorphism $\varpi$ with $\varpi \circ \varpi = [r]$ and $\varpi \circ \mathrm{act}(a) = \mathrm{act}(\varphi a) \circ \varpi$), formal coordinates $\theta_0$ of $A_0.f$ in two variables, families $e,e' : \Gamma_t \to \operatorname{End}(A_0.A)$ with $e\gamma$ lying over the structure morphism (the witnesses `he`), and a function $\deg : \Gamma_t \to \mathbb N$, such that all of the following hold.
--
--   (1) $X_0$ has height $4$: the kernel of $\mathrm{act}(r)$ on $X_0$ is finite and projective over $k_0$ and has rank $r^4$ in every field fibre.
--
--   (2) `A₀.IsFormalModuleVia coord X₀ θ₀`: the maps $\theta_0$ are formal coordinates for the group law of $A_0$ with respect to $X_0.F$ (naturality in the test algebra, infinitesimality, injectivity and surjectivity onto the $J$-infinitesimal points, and compatibility of $F$'s truncated multiplication with $L$), and for every commutative $k_0$-algebra $B'$, every ideal $J$ with $J^{m+1} = 0$, every $m \in \Lambda$ and every $s \in J^2$, the coordinate $\theta_0$ of the series $\mathrm{addVia}\,F\,(\mathrm{act}(\mathrm{coord}(m)_1))\,(\mathrm{act}(\mathrm{coord}(m)_2) \circ \varpi)$ evaluated at $s$ is the image of $\theta_0 B' s$ under $\mathrm{act}(m)$.
--
--   (3) For every $\gamma \in \Gamma_t$: $(e\gamma, e'\gamma)$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{\deg \gamma}$ (both are homomorphisms for the group laws, both commute with the $\Lambda$-action, and their two composites are $\mathrm{act}(r^{\deg \gamma})$ whenever $r^{\deg\gamma} \in \Lambda$), and $e\gamma$ preserves the level structure (points factoring through `lev` are carried to points factoring through `lev`).
--
--   (4) For all $\gamma,\gamma' \in \Gamma_t$ there are $i,j \in \mathbb N$ such that $e(\gamma\gamma')$ followed by $\mathrm{act}(r^i)$ equals $e(\gamma')$ followed by $e(\gamma)$ followed by $\mathrm{act}(r^j)$.
--
--   (5) If $\gamma \in \Gamma_t$ is the rational-integer scalar $c \cdot 1$, then for some $i$, $e\gamma$ followed by $\mathrm{act}(r^i)$ equals $\mathrm{act}(c\,r^i)$.
--
--   (6) Conversely, if for some $\gamma \in \Gamma_t$ there are $i$ and $c > 0$ in $\mathbb N$ with $e\gamma$ followed by $\mathrm{act}(r^i)$ equal to $\mathrm{act}(c)$, then $\gamma$ is a rational scalar: $\gamma = c' \cdot 1$ for some $c' \in \mathbb Q$.
--
--   (7) (Endomorphism dictionary.) For every injective ring homomorphism $E_0$ from the centraliser of $\{X_0.\mathrm{actEnd}(a)\} \cup \{X_0.\varpi\mathrm{End}\}$ inside the endomorphisms of $X_0.F$ into $M_2(K_0)$, there is $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ such that every $\gamma \in \Gamma_t$ admits $\varepsilon$ in that centraliser and $k_\gamma \in \mathbb Z$ with: for every commutative $k_0$-algebra $B'$, ideal $J$ with $J^{m+1} = 0$ and $s \in J^2$, the value of $\theta_0$ at the truncated power series of $\varepsilon$ evaluated at $s$ equals the image of $\theta_0 B' s$ under $e\gamma$; and $E_0(\varepsilon) = r^{k_\gamma}\, g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$.
--
--   (8) (Labelling on full level-$n$ structures.) For every full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ on $A_0$ (a section $P$ with $nP = 0$ whose $\Lambda$-orbit is all of the geometric $n$-torsion and whose annihilator in $\Lambda$ is exactly $n\Lambda$) there is $\mathrm{lab} : \Gamma_t \to \Lambda$ with: $e\gamma$ carries $P$ to $\mathrm{act}(\mathrm{lab}\,\gamma)$ applied to $P$, for all $\gamma$; $\mathrm{lab}(\gamma\gamma') - \mathrm{lab}(\gamma')\,\mathrm{lab}(\gamma) \in n\Lambda$ for all $\gamma,\gamma'$; and if $\gamma = c \cdot 1$ with $c \in \mathbb Z$ then $\mathrm{lab}\,\gamma - c \cdot 1 \in n\Lambda$.
--
--   (9) (Exhaustion of isogenies of $r$-power degree.) For every algebraically closed field $k$ that is a $k_0$-algebra, every fake elliptic curve $A$ over $k$ of type `FakeEllipticCurve Λ N k` and every $g_A : A.A \to A_0.A$ exhibiting $A$ as the pullback of $A_0$ along $k_0 \to k$ (`IsPullbackVia`: a pullback square compatible with the group laws, commuting with the $\Lambda$-actions, and carrying level-factoring points to level-factoring points), and for all endomorphisms $\varphi,\psi$ of $A.A$ with $\varphi$ over the structure morphism, $(\varphi,\psi)$ an isogeny pair of degree $r^d$ and $\varphi$ level-preserving, there are $\gamma \in \Gamma_t$ and $i,j \in \mathbb N$ such that $\varphi$ followed by $\mathrm{act}(r^i)$ followed by $g_A$ equals $g_A$ followed by $e\gamma$ followed by $\mathrm{act}(r^j)$.
--
--   (10) (Endomorphism-ring export.) There exist a $\mathbb Z$-submodule $R_2 \leq R_1$ which is an order, such that every $x \in R_1$ has $r^c x \in R_2$ for some $c \in \mathbb N$, and a family $\hat e : R_2 \to \operatorname{End}(A_0.A)$ with each $\hat e\,x$ over the structure morphism, satisfying:
--
--   • each $\hat e\,x$ is a homomorphism for the group law $L$, commutes with $\mathrm{act}(m)$ for all $m \in \Lambda$, and preserves the level structure;
--    • $\hat e\,1 = \mathrm{id}$ (when $1 \in R_2$); $\hat e\,(xy) = \hat e\,y$ followed by $\hat e\,x$ (when $xy \in R_2$); $\hat e$ of a rational integer $m$ is $\mathrm{act}(m)$;
--    • if $y = \bar x$ and $\mathrm{nrd}(x)$ is the integer $n_x$, then $\hat e\,y$ followed by $\hat e\,x$ is $\mathrm{act}(n_x)$;
--    • compatibility with $e$: if $x \in R_2$ equals $r^k \gamma$ for some $\gamma \in \Gamma_t$ and $k \in \mathbb N$, then for some $i,j$, $e\gamma$ followed by $\mathrm{act}(r^i)$ equals $\hat e\,x$ followed by $\mathrm{act}(r^j)$;
--    • a joint dictionary: for every injective ring homomorphism $E_0$ from the centraliser of the $\mathrm{actEnd}$-image together with $\varpi\mathrm{End}$ into $M_2(K_0)$ there is a single $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ such that every $\gamma \in \Gamma_t$ has $\varepsilon, k_\gamma$ inducing $e\gamma$ on formal coordinates with $E_0(\varepsilon) = r^{k_\gamma} g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$, and every $x \in R_2$ has $\varepsilon, k_x$ inducing $\hat e\,x$ on formal coordinates with $E_0(\varepsilon) = r^{k_x} g\,\iota_0(x)\,g^{-1}$;
--    • $R_2$ is stable under the quaternionic conjugation $\mathrm{star}$;
--    • and (twin order) there exist a maximal order $\Lambda_1^s$ containing $R_1$ with $\Lambda_1 \cap \Lambda_1^s = R_1$, an order $R_2' \leq \Lambda_1^s$ with $R_2 \leq R_2'$ such that every $z \in \Lambda_1^s$ has $r^c z \in R_2'$ for some $c$, and a family $\hat e' : R_2' \to \operatorname{End}(A_0.A)$ over the structure morphism, such that: each $\hat e'\,z$ is a homomorphism for $L$ and commutes with the $\Lambda$-action; $\hat e'\,1 = \mathrm{id}$, $\hat e'(zy) = \hat e'\,y$ followed by $\hat e'\,z$, $\hat e'$ of a rational integer $m$ is $\mathrm{act}(m)$; $\hat e'(\bar z)$ followed by $\hat e'\,z$ is $\mathrm{act}(\mathrm{nrd}\,z)$ when $\mathrm{nrd}\,z$ is an integer; $\hat e'$ restricts to $\hat e$ on $R_2$; $R_2'$ is stable under $\mathrm{star}$; $\hat e'$ is injective; and for every prime $q \neq r$ and every $z \in R_2'$, if $\hat e'\,z$ annihilates all $q$-torsion points (for every test base and every point $P$ with $qP$ the identity, $\hat e'\,z$ sends $P$ to the identity), then $r^K z = q\,y$ for some $K \in \mathbb N$ and some $y \in \Lambda_1^s$.
--
--   The clause on level preservation appears for $\hat e$ but not for $\hat e'$, and injectivity is asserted for $\hat e'$ only.
--
--   This produces the supersingular base point of the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison over an algebraic closure of $\mathbb F_r$: a fake elliptic curve with $\Lambda$-action and level-$N$ structure, its formal $\mathcal O_D$-module of height $4$, the action of $R_1[1/r]^\times$ on it up to powers of $r$, and the translation ("dictionary") between endomorphisms of the formal module and matrices $g\,\iota_0(\cdot)\,g^{-1}$ in $M_2(K_0)$, together with an export of the full $r$-power-saturated Eichler order and of its twin. It is the geometric input to the fine-level Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation statement [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.exists_cerednikDrinfeld_uniformization_fine_level_atkinLehner_minusT_liftT_of_squarefree_of_isUnit_two_of_pow_smul_mem`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.exists_cerednikDrinfeld_uniformization_fine_level_atkinLehner_minusT_liftT_of_squarefree_of_isUnit_two_of_pow_smul_mem), and the hypothesis that $2$ is invertible in the base is carried along for the canonical-polarisation step.
